# Operations-Analyst Brief — Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals

**Run:** `quiet-by-design-standards-for-mwaa-terminals-2`
**Author lens:** Operations-analyst (throughput, PA and paging performance, IROPS messaging, commissioning verification).
**Rule of engagement:** Independent brief. I have not read the other Stage 1 briefs. This document is the operational reality check on the thesis — where an MWAA "quiet-by-design" standard survives contact with how terminals actually run, and where it does not.

---

## 1. Bottom line up front

The peer evidence supports the thesis that quiet-by-design is real, measurable, and does not degrade departure performance — but only when the "quiet" is engineered, not proclaimed. The airports that report the largest quantitative gains built quiet on four load-bearing pieces at once: zoned PA hardware that physically prevents building-wide broadcast, room acoustics tuned so any announcement that *is* made is understood the first time, visual and digital redundancy that carries the load the PA used to carry, and a governance layer that removes the discretion to page from the people who used to have it. Remove any one leg and the policy collapses back into announcements-plus-signage. That is the operational thesis MWAA needs to defend at commissioning, not at press release.

## 2. Key findings

1. **Dubai reduced daily announcements from over 2,000 across three terminals to under 700 with "no impact on departure performance" — a 65% cut,** attributed by CEO Paul Griffiths to a two-per-flight cap negotiated with airlines over ten months [Source: https://media.dubaiairports.ae/dubai-international-gives-passengers-the-silent-treatment/]. That is the largest single-hub reduction with an on-time-performance claim attached in any publicly reported peer case.
2. **SFO reduced individual paging events from 492 to 261 (a 47% cut) and cumulative paging duration from 145 to 58 minutes per day (a 60% cut) after physically unplugging airline access to zone-wide paging** and forcing gate calls into the immediate gate area [Source: https://www.airport-technology.com/features/san-francisco-quiet-airport/]. The often-quoted "40% noise reduction" and "over 90 minutes/day eliminated" numbers are SFO Customer Care staff estimates, not independently metered — treat as directional [Source: https://www.airportxnews.com/sfo-reduces-in-terminal-noise-levels/].
3. **The ACRP survey found the average measured reverberation time at 2 kHz in airport concourses was 1.8 seconds (range 0.8–3.4 s), against a voice-intelligibility target near 0.5 s,** and 69% of industry respondents ranked high reverberation as the single most important factor hindering PA intelligibility [Source: https://airportscouncil.org/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/2018Annual-bit-session9-Glickman.pdf]. In plain terms, the room is the failure mode. You cannot page your way out of a stone-and-glass concourse.
4. **The IEC 60268-16 Speech Transmission Index (STI) provides a defensible, contract-writable metric** — 0.50 is the widely applied minimum for public paging, 0.60 is "good," and NFPA 72 requires voice notification to be intelligible within each Acoustically Distinguishable Space [Source: https://www.commercialintegrator.com/sponsored/mass-notification-intelligibility/76485/; https://acousplan.com/blog/iec-60268-16-sti-guide]. STI is what MWAA should specify and what commissioning agents should be paid to prove, room by room.
5. **London City Airport has operated a "silent terminal" policy since 2008 with a defined exceptions list (gate changes at short notice, IROPS, emergencies) and reports favorable passenger environment scores — but publishes no missed-boarding data** [Source: https://www.holidayextras.com/airport-parking/london-city/on-airport/silent-terminal.html; https://airport.nridigital.com/air_mar18/shhhh_is_the_day_of_the_airport_announcer_over]. The most useful lesson from LCY is not that it went quiet; it is that the exceptions carve-out is doing more work than the policy.
6. **Helsinki-Vantaa's Finavia study cited by peer press concluded that passengers found the quiet policy "more positive than neutral" and that wayfinding effects were "neutral"** — i.e., people did not report worse navigation without announcements [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_mar18/shhhh_is_the_day_of_the_airport_announcer_over]. The primary Finavia report is not accessible; the finding should be attributed as a secondary characterization until verified.
7. **The ambient sound floor in a typical U.S. airport terminal averages roughly 66 dBA, and industry references target a 12–15 dB signal-to-noise ratio for airport PA** [Source: https://ezobord.com/acoustic-design-public-spaces/; https://pro.harman.com/insights/jbl-pro/loudspeaker-system-design-for-airports-ensuring-everyone-gets-the-message/]. That framing is what makes zone-first PA operationally rational: if you must broadcast at 78 dBA to overcome the room, the cheapest way to lower announcement loudness is to shrink the audience.
8. **The airports whose quiet policy operates without degrading throughput share three engineered choices**: (a) PA zoning at the gate-cluster or single-gate level rather than concourse or terminal, (b) a hard cap on airline discretionary paging with a residual channel held by airport communications staff, and (c) high-density, high-resolution FIDS and app coverage as the primary information carrier. That is the pattern that shows up at SFO, DXB, LCY, Schiphol, and Helsinki. Any MWAA standard that specifies only one of the three should be understood as adopting the aesthetic of a quiet airport without the operational substance.

## 3. Evidence — how peer airports actually implement quiet

### Dubai International (DXB)

DXB's own communications office reports the initiative "cut the number of daily announcements from over 2000 across all three terminals to less than 700" and that this was accomplished "with no impact on departure performance." The mechanism was governance more than technology: Paul Griffiths, CEO, is quoted saying "Over the past 10 months we have worked closely with our customer airlines to limit announcements to a maximum of two per flight" [Source: https://media.dubaiairports.ae/dubai-international-gives-passengers-the-silent-treatment/]. The DXB material does not disclose the airline-side pushback structure, IROPS-day behavior, or how the two-per-flight cap interacts with off-schedule operations. Those are the operational stress cases that break a cap, and MWAA cannot borrow DXB's number without borrowing DXB's enforcement.

### SFO Quiet Airport

The SFO program is the most operationally instructive because it names the intervention that made the numbers move. Christopher Birch, Director of Guest Experience, describes the mechanism: "We physically unplugged [carriers'] ability to make boarding area-wide paging" and "worked to create individual zones that allowed it to push pages out to relevant areas only" — with the residual "final calls" channel handed to SFO communications staff rather than left with the airline [Source: https://www.airport-technology.com/features/san-francisco-quiet-airport/]. The measured outcome — 492 to 261 individual paging occurrences (47%), and 145 to 58 minutes of cumulative paging time (60%) — comes from that same source. Note that the more famous "over 90 minutes/day eliminated" and "over 40% noise reduction" figures trace to internal Customer Care estimates rather than to a metered acoustic survey [Source: https://www.airportxnews.com/sfo-reduces-in-terminal-noise-levels/]. Both classes of number should be reported, and both should be attributed honestly. Birch also names the pressure point MWAA will meet on day one: "We have Americans with Disabilities Act requirements here and that's going to be the tough one to incorporate."

### London City, Schiphol, Helsinki-Vantaa, Copenhagen

London City has run a silent-terminal policy since 2008 with a defined exceptions list — gate changes at short notice, IROPS, and emergencies — and integrates real-time gate information via FIDS and messaging apps [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_mar18/shhhh_is_the_day_of_the_airport_announcer_over; https://www.holidayextras.com/airport-parking/london-city/on-airport/silent-terminal.html]. Amsterdam Schiphol communicates the operating model as "virtually no announcements," with FIDS and the Schiphol app as the primary information channel [Source: https://www.vanemag.com/silent-airports-list-2026/]. Helsinki-Vantaa was moved to a silent operating model by Finavia beginning June 2019 [Source: https://morepremium.com/helsinki-vantaa-to-be-made-a-quiet-airport/]. Copenhagen Airport is described as a silent-airport operator with minimized voice announcements and passenger-monitored FIDS [Source: https://www.vanemag.com/silent-airports-list-2026/]. None of these peer sources publishes missed-boarding statistics — an evidence gap the brief should not paper over.

### The operational pattern

Across the five peer cases, the successful implementations share four attributes: physical PA zoning at gate-cluster or gate level, a governance cap on airline discretionary paging (SFO removed the access; DXB negotiated the cap; LCY defines the exceptions list), high-density visual information (FIDS everywhere the passenger can plausibly be), and a digital push channel (app, SMS, or messenger). Where an airport has only two of the four, the "silent" label is aesthetic. Where an airport has three, the quiet is real but fragile under IROPS. Where an airport has all four, quiet survives IROPS because the residual voice channel is narrow, credible, and reserved for genuinely urgent information.

## 4. Evidence — what a measurable standard has to contain

The Speech Transmission Index (IEC 60268-16, most recent edition 2020) is the metric a design manual can enforce. STI is a single number between 0 and 1 that folds together background noise and reverberation and yields a value the commissioning agent can measure with a STIPA test set [Source: https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/26771; https://acousplan.com/blog/iec-60268-16-sti-guide]. The industry convention treats STI ≥ 0.50 as the minimum for public paging, 0.60+ as good, and below 0.44 as poor [Source: https://pro.harman.com/insights/jbl-pro/loudspeaker-system-design-for-airports-ensuring-everyone-gets-the-message/]. NFPA 72 requires voice notification to be intelligible within each Acoustically Distinguishable Space and treats intelligibility as a designer-declared, verified property rather than a single number — the practical effect is that emergency voice systems in most public buildings are engineered to STI ≈ 0.50 or higher [Source: https://www.commercialintegrator.com/sponsored/mass-notification-intelligibility/76485/; https://www.ecmag.com/magazine/articles/article-detail/integrated-systems-use-your-inside-voice-intelligibility-requirements-fire-alarm-systems].

The room acoustic that lets that STI be achieved is not a nice-to-have — it is the design variable that dominates. ACRP Report 175 measured airport terminals and found average reverberation time at 2 kHz was 1.6 seconds overall and 1.8 seconds in concourses (range 0.8 s to 3.4 s), against a voice-intelligibility target that begins to work near 0.5 s [Source: https://airportscouncil.org/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/2018Annual-bit-session9-Glickman.pdf]. Sixty-nine percent of the ACRP industry survey respondents named reverberation as the top intelligibility-limiting factor in their terminals. Typical daytime ambient noise in a U.S. terminal averages ~66 dBA, and TSA and paging content needs to sit ~12–15 dB above that floor to be understood [Source: https://ezobord.com/acoustic-design-public-spaces/].

A defensible MWAA standard should therefore specify, at minimum, four measurable properties per acoustically distinguishable space: (i) an STI floor of 0.50 for general paging and 0.60 within emergency notification zones, verified by STIPA at commissioning; (ii) a reverberation time (T30) target expressed as a curve across the speech octaves, not a single mid-band value; (iii) a background LAeq design ceiling that recognizes that if the room is measured at 70 dBA in normal ops, the PA must be capable of overcoming it without pain; and (iv) an adjacent-zone spillover limit expressed in dB attenuation across gate-cluster boundaries. None of these are exotic. All four are already routine in performance-based audio commissioning.

## 5. Evidence — what the IAD and DCA operating environment imposes

Two operating realities constrain the standard. First, IAD's international share (10.53M of 29.01M — roughly 36% — in 2025) makes non-native-speaker intelligibility a first-order design driver, and non-native listeners require materially higher STI than native listeners to achieve the same comprehension [Source: https://pro.harman.com/insights/jbl-pro/loudspeaker-system-design-for-airports-ensuring-everyone-gets-the-message/]. A generic 0.50 floor is not enough at IAD for messages that must be understood in a hurry; a working design should push STI ≥ 0.55–0.60 in international gate clusters, and should require multilingual pre-recorded scripts where messages are safety-relevant, which is consistent with NFPA 72's own multilingual guidance for populations that require it [Source: https://www.ecmag.com/magazine/articles/article-detail/integrated-systems-use-your-inside-voice-intelligibility-requirements-fire-alarm-systems].

Second, DCA post-January 2025 lives with more prescribed IROPS messaging than any US slot-controlled airport of its size. The FAA restricted Route 4 and helicopter operations near runways 15/33 and 4/22 in March 2025 following the midair collision, which added recurring runway and airspace notifications to what was already a slot-controlled, business-traveler-heavy operation carrying gate-change frequency well above the domestic median (the passenger context in `airport-context.md`). DCA cannot adopt a quiet standard that leaves the operator without a well-trained residual voice channel; the peer pattern that survives is not "no voice" — it is "narrow voice, on demand, held by airport communications, not gate agents."

Third, both airports are approaching the moment where the standards decision is real. Concourse E opens fall 2026 — the specifications for its PA, ceiling absorption, and FIDS density are already frozen. The $20B IAD transformation with Concourse C/D replacement, and the DCA Terminal 1 concourse replacement, are the projects that will actually inherit whatever standard MWAA adopts (the capital-program context in `airport-context.md`). A standards decision made in 2026 has ~24 months to make it into the C/D and T1 procurement documents in a non-change-order form.

## 6. Counterexamples — where operations could not solve the problem

A rigorous brief has to name where the peer airports found that *operational* changes alone did not deliver the outcome and infrastructure or specification changes had to carry it.

- **SFO required physical hardware intervention, not policy alone.** Christopher Birch's phrasing — "we physically unplugged" airline access to zone-wide paging — matters because SFO had already tried announcement-content guidance and had gotten drift back to old habits. Removing the capability was the durable fix [Source: https://www.airport-technology.com/features/san-francisco-quiet-airport/]. Operational discipline could not survive shift changes and airline-side turnover. The infrastructure change did.
- **DXB required a ten-month airline-by-airline negotiation, not a fiat.** Paul Griffiths's own quote ("we have worked closely with our customer airlines to limit announcements to a maximum of two per flight") describes governance and lease infrastructure, not overnight policy [Source: https://media.dubaiairports.ae/dubai-international-gives-passengers-the-silent-treatment/]. The MWAA equivalent — amending airline use-and-lease and tenant technology standards — is a Board-level move, not an ops memo.
- **The ACRP finding that 69% of respondents blame reverberation** is a direct statement that operations cannot outrun the room [Source: https://airportscouncil.org/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/2018Annual-bit-session9-Glickman.pdf]. Retrofitting ceiling absorption, sound-attenuating floor and wall treatments, and gate-hold acoustic separation are capital moves. Post-occupancy, they cost multiples of what they would have cost as specified line items in a new-build procurement.
- **ADA and DOT accessibility requirements are absorbed by visual infrastructure, not by removing the voice channel.** Announcements may be provided in visual format via electronic message boards or video paging (BWI is cited as an example), and the DOT is actively considering extending assistive-listening requirements to transportation facilities [Source: https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2006/02/23/06-1656/accommodations-for-individuals-who-are-deaf-hard-of-hearing-or-deaf-blind; https://www.access-board.gov/ada/vehicles/appendix-advisory-guidance/]. That means "quiet" only works if visual and assistive-listening density is *higher* than in a conventional terminal — not equal to it. This is an infrastructure spec, not an operating practice.
- **Sensory rooms and low-stimulation zones require room-scale acoustic construction, not policy.** ACRP's own facility-accessibility guidance recommends dedicated quiet or sensory space in each concourse, with high-NRC ceilings, additional sound insulation, carpet, and soft lighting — physical scope, not a labeled corner [Source: https://crp.trb.org/acrpwebresource14/facility-accessibility/; https://aci.aero/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SEA-Sensory-Room-Case-Study-2024-06-04.pdf].

