## 8. Evidence gaps worth flagging to Stage 2

- No primary source verifies missed-boarding rate change at any silent-airport peer.
- SFO's headline "40% noise reduction" is an internal estimate, not a metered survey.
- ACRP Report 175 full text was not directly retrievable in this Stage 1 sprint (PDF encoding failure at both TRB and Wilson Ihrig endpoints); the specific numerical values cited come from the ACRP presentation deck and reputable secondary summaries. Stage 2 should treat any strategic quote against Report 175 as requiring page-level verification.
- The 2020 MWAA Design Manual was not read directly for this brief. Whether it already contains STI, T30, or LAeq acoustic thresholds is unknown, and whether it does or does not is the single most important gating question for whether "quiet-by-design" is a new standard or an amendment.
- Peer-airport primary documentation for LCY, HEL, CPH, and BCN was not directly retrieved; those airports' policies are characterized here from published policy pages and industry press. The final article should not quote peer specifics without confirmed primary sources.

## 9. Professional judgment (my read, not sourced to a single document)

Three judgments belong in this brief rather than in the evidence file, because they are experience-based reads on how airport operations actually behave under a standard like this:

- **A quiet standard imposed without gate-agent workflow redesign will fail within six months.** Gate agents whose incentive structures still count boarding-completion time will reintroduce zone paging via handhelds, podium mics, or in-plain-view workarounds. The standard has to reach airline gate SOPs and the airline recovery model, or the airport will silently absorb the missed-boarding risk at the operations control center.
- **DCA and IAD require different implementations, not different policies.** The mandatory layer (STI floor per ADS, T30 curve, adjacent-zone spillover limit, FIDS density per gate cluster, sensory room per concourse, tenant PA and TV governance) should be common. The variable layer (multilingual scripting depth, IROPS residual voice frequency, gate-cluster zone size, curb-to-gate visual reinforcement) should be tuned to the traffic profile of each airport. A one-page "differences table" in the Design Manual will do more for enforceability than a hundred pages of common prose.
- **Commissioning is the point where the standard lives or dies.** Every peer that made the numbers move engineered a post-installation verification step. MWAA should treat STIPA verification, adjacent-zone spillover measurement, and a functional IROPS voice test as commissioning line items with contractual acceptance criteria — not as designer good-faith assurances. If commissioning is not paid for, the standard is not real.

